- Retrieved practices:
- Verify that input validation is enforced on a trusted service layer.

- Verify that the application protects against template injection attacks by ensuring that any user input being included is sanitized or sandboxed.

- MEM31-C: Free dynamically allocated memory when no longer needed
WHAT: Failing to call free() on dynamically allocated memory (e.g., from malloc()) before the lifetime of the last pointer to it ends causes memory to remain permanently allocated (memory leak).
WHY: Memory leaks can exhaust available memory, leading to degraded performance, denial of service, or process termination over time.
HOW: For every successful call to a standard allocation function (malloc, calloc, realloc), ensure a corresponding free() is invoked on the same pointer before all references to it go out of scope or are overwritten; structure code so every exit path (including error paths) frees owned allocations.
EXAMPLE:
Noncompliant: `char *buf = malloc(N); if (!buf) return -1; return 0;`
Compliant: `char *buf = malloc(N); if (!buf) return -1; /* use buf */ free(buf); return 0;`

- Free dynamically allocated memory when no longer needed
WHAT: Dynamically allocated memory must be freed when it is no longer needed, except when it is intentionally stored in a pointer with static storage duration that is used for the entire program execution.
WHY: Failing to free unneeded heap memory causes memory leaks, which can exhaust memory, degrade performance, or lead to denial-of-service conditions.
HOW: Track ownership of each malloc/realloc/calloc allocation and call free() exactly once when the data is no longer required; only omit free() when the allocation is intentionally tied to a static-lifetime pointer for the whole program run.
EXAMPLE (C, before): `char *buf = malloc(32); if (!buf) return -1; /* use buf */ return 0; /* buf never freed */`
EXAMPLE (C, after): `static char *buf = NULL; if (!buf) { buf = malloc(32); if (!buf) return -1; } /* reuse buf for entire program; no free needed */`

- Correct use of errno in C error handling
WHAT: Misusing errno by reading it without first checking a function’s failure return value, or by not resetting errno to 0 before calling functions that may set it, can cause incorrect error detection and handling.
WHY: Stale or unrelated errno values can lead to misreported errors, skipped error handling, or incorrect program behavior, potentially masking real failures.
HOW: For functions documented to set errno on error, first check their return value for failure; only if it indicates failure, then inspect errno. When using errno to detect errors for functions without a unique error return, set errno to 0 immediately before the call, then check errno only after the call completes.
EXAMPLE: Before: `f = fopen(path, "r"); if (errno == ENOENT) { ... }`
After: `errno = 0; f = fopen(path, "r"); if (f == NULL) { if (errno == ENOENT) { ... } }`

- Detect and handle standard library errors
WHAT: Failing to check or incorrectly checking standard library function return values (including NULL returns) can leave errors undetected or unhandled.
WHY: Unhandled errors can cause undefined behavior, crashes (e.g., NULL pointer dereference), security bypasses, or data corruption.
HOW: For every standard library call that can fail, always check its documented return value or error indicator, handle all failure cases explicitly, and avoid using results (pointers, sizes, descriptors) until they are validated.
EXAMPLE (C): Before: `char *p = malloc(n); memcpy(p, src, n);` After: `char *p = malloc(n); if (p == NULL) { /* handle error */ return ERR; } memcpy(p, src, n);`



